## THE OTHER DIRECTION: does the epsilon over-correct?

Weighted equally, because an over-eager epsilon under-recommends and sends the
user to move too little. It does NOT over-correct. I measured the absorption
band directly rather than trusting the fixture list.

Probe A (`TestProbe_GenuineOneCentOvershootSweep`, my own file, scratch only):
for every cent c in [0,100), `start = 800.00+c/100`, `outflow = 400.01+c/100`,
so the true decimal shortfall is exactly $100.01 — one genuine cent past the
boundary:

    genuine $100.01 shortfalls correctly rounded to 200: 100/100 (wrong: 0)
    --- PASS

Probe B (`TestProbe2_AbsorptionThreshold`) walks the boundary to find where the
epsilon stops absorbing:

    roundUpToHundred(100+0           ) = 100.0000
    roundUpToHundred(100+9.948e-14   ) = 100.0000     <- observed dust magnitude
    roundUpToHundred(100+1e-09       ) = 100.0000
    roundUpToHundred(100+1e-08       ) = 100.0000
    roundUpToHundred(100+9e-08       ) = 100.0000
    roundUpToHundred(100+1.1e-07     ) = 200.0000     <- absorption ends here
    roundUpToHundred(100+1e-06       ) = 200.0000
    roundUpToHundred(100+0.001       ) = 200.0000     <- a tenth of a cent
    roundUpToHundred(100+0.01        ) = 200.0000     <- one cent, REAL money

The epsilon is `1e-9` in quotient space = `1e-7` dollars = 0.00001 cents. So the
three magnitudes are cleanly separated by many orders:

    float dust observed:   5.7e-14 .. 2.3e-13 dollars
    epsilon absorbs up to: 1.0e-7  dollars     (~5x10^5 above the worst dust)
    smallest real money:   1.0e-2  dollars     (~1x10^5 above the epsilon)

The epsilon sits roughly in the geometric middle. It cannot swallow a cent, and
it cannot fail to swallow the dust. `TestRoundUpToHundred_GenuinelyPastBoundaryStillRoundsUp`
(projection_test.go:612-628) pins 100.01, 100.50, 199.99, 200.01 in-tree, and it
PASSES both pre-fix and post-fix — correctly, since it is a guard against
over-correction, not a discriminator for the defect.

