## Ruling 2026-08-16e — loop assertions flagged (observations, not failures)

I am flagging these as the ruling requires. None is a failure, because in every
case the iteration count is fixed in the source of the same function and cannot
be zero at runtime — no loop here ranges over a value derived from production
code, so none can be vacuous.

  - projection_test.go:519 `TestRoundUpToHundred` — ranges an 8-element literal
    table declared at :506-518. No preceding count assertion.
  - projection_test.go:552 `TestRoundUpToHundred_CentDustAtExactBoundary` —
    ranges a 5-element literal table declared at :542-551. No count assertion.
  - projection_test.go:622 `TestRoundUpToHundred_GenuinelyPastBoundaryStillRoundsUp` —
    ranges a 4-element literal table declared at :613-621. No count assertion.
  - projection_test.go:571 / :591 — index loops `for c := 0; c < 100; c++`;
    the bound is a literal, so 100 iterations are guaranteed.
  - projection_test.go:431 `TestProject_DoesNotMutateInputs` — `for i := range
    anchorsBefore` with NO preceding length assertion, whereas the sibling `txs`
    loop (:436) and the `recurring` check (:444) both DO assert length first.
    This is the one real inconsistency in the file. It is harmless
    (`anchorsBefore` copies a 2-element literal, and `acct` is passed by value so
    its slice header cannot change in the caller) but a
    `len(acct.Anchors) != len(anchorsBefore)` check would make it match its own
    neighbours.

## Additional observation (not a defect, recorded for the file)

`roundUpToHundred` returns negative zero for `x` in [1e-9, 1e-7]:

    roundUpToHundred(1e-09) = -0.0000  negzero=true
    roundUpToHundred(5e-08) = -0.0000  negzero=true
    roundUpToHundred(1e-07) = -0.0000  negzero=true
    roundUpToHundred(2e-07) = 100.0000

`math.Ceil` of a small negative quotient yields -0. This band is unreachable from
`Project`: the crossing gate at projection.go:154 requires
`balance < Threshold - 1e-9`, and any shortfall arising from cent-valued data is
either >= $0.01 or is float dust below `floatTolerance` (caught by the
`x < floatTolerance` guard at :238, which returns a positive 0). It would take a
shortfall of under a ten-millionth of a dollar to reach it. `moneyEq(-0, 0)` is
true, so no assertion is affected. Cosmetic only; no action needed.

