## Conclusion

The one-line fix is correct in both directions and, unlike attempt 1, is now
covered by tests that actually discriminate: reverting the single changed line in
an out-of-repo scratch copy flips four tests from PASS to FAIL, including both
fixtures named in the brief and one end-to-end test through the real `Project`
wiring. The epsilon absorbs up to 1e-7 dollars — five orders of magnitude above
the worst observed dust (2.3e-13) and five orders below one cent — so it cannot
under-recommend on a genuine cent-level overshoot; 100 of 100 synthetic $100.01
shortfalls still round to $200. Items 1-5 and 7-10 were re-verified independently
this attempt (not carried over) and all hold, including transaction-level account
isolation and the unknown-vs-zero distinction, which I proved by execution rather
than by reading. Toolchain is fully green: build, vet, staticcheck, gofmt clean,
and `go test ./...` rc=0 across 47 packages.

Repository state on completion: `git status --porcelain` shows no tracked source
file modified. The only untracked entries under `.swarm/verdicts/` are verdict
files. All scratch work lived in
`/tmp/claude-1000/-home-darrell-work-agents2/e77cc9b5-a7ea-4593-a19e-1669b194a03a/scratchpad/prefix-repo`,
outside the repository, and my probe test files were never written into the repo.

VERDICT: PASS
